--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

The data is licensed separately

The MIT license above covers the code: the Rust crates, the web shell, and the
build and data-prep scripts.

The text and the lexical data are other people's work, redistributed here under
their own terms. Those terms travel with the files; nothing above changes them.

  data/     the KJV 1769 text and the 1769 translators' margin notes (public
            domain — a Crown patent applies in the UK); the American King James
            Version overlay (public domain); Strong's Hebrew and Greek
            dictionaries from Open Scriptures (CC-BY-SA); OSHB Hebrew
            morphology (CC-BY 4.0); Robinson's Greek parsing codes (public
            domain); Treasury of Scripture Knowledge cross-references via
            openbible.info (CC Attribution; the TSK itself is public domain).

  bridge/   Abbott-Smith's Manual Greek Lexicon (public-domain text, from a
            CC-BY-SA repository); the LXX alignment (Swete's LXX text is public
            domain; see the upstream repositories for the rest); STEPBible's
            TIPNR proper-name identities (CC-BY 4.0).

  fonts     EB Garamond, under the SIL Open Font License. OFL.txt ships beside
            the fonts in apps/web/public/fonts/.

The files built from those sources inherit whatever they were derived from —
data/kjv.jsonl.idxcache, data/akjv.akjvb, data/source-priors.json,
data/quotation-pairs.json, data/text-witness.json, and the packed forms the
shell loads. The shell bundles copies of the data (under
apps/web/public/pack/); the same terms follow the copies.

Everything else is MIT, as above: the code, the authored study set (threads,
tags, weaves), and data/concept-vectors.vec (+ .meta, .freq), which is trained
here over Strong's numbers alone.

BIBLIOGRAPHY.md has the per-file detail — every source, its upstream repository,
and its license.
